**Definition 3.1 (the thirteen equations).** Index
$j=(\sigma,\eta)$ in the order $(++),(+-),(-+),(--)$ and put

$$
\begin{gathered}
\varphi(d)=\sqrt{1-2\gamma d-\gamma d^2},\quad
u_j(d)=\sigma(1+d),\quad v_j(d)=\eta\varphi(d),\\
m_j(d)=(1,u_j(d),v_j(d),u_j(d)v_j(d),u_j(d)^2),\quad
f_j(d)=f(u_j(d),v_j(d)),\\
k_j(d)=0\quad(j\ne(--)),\qquad k_{--}(d)=-f_{--}(d).
\end{gathered}
\tag{EP.19}
$$

The square root is the positive analytic branch near zero. Strict corner
signs make $k_j$ the local analytic expression of $[-f]_+$.
For $a=(a_0,a_1,a_2,a_3,a_4)$ define
$P_a(u,v)=a_0+a_1u+a_2v+a_3uv+a_4u^2$. For a real parameter $p$
solve for four $\pi_j$, four $d_j$ and five $a_i$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\sum_j\pi_jm_j(d_j)&=(1,0,0,0,1),\\
a\cdot m_j(d_j)&=1+d_j+p k_j(d_j)\quad\text{for every }j,\\
a\cdot m_j'(d_j)&=1+p k_j'(d_j)\quad\text{for every }j,
\end{aligned}
\tag{EP.20}
$$

selecting the limit

$$
\pi_j(0)=1/4,\quad d_j(0)=0,\quad
 a(0)=(1/2,0,0,0,1/2).
\tag{EP.21}
$$

The scalar $p$ is an analytic parameter, not a source prior, specimen
coordinate or acquired probability. A prime on $m_j,k_j$ denotes its
scalar $d$ derivative.

